# Line endings. One rule for the whole tree, because the alternative costs a
# review every time: an editor or a zip made on another machine leaves CRLF in
# the working tree, and the files without a `text` attribute then show up as
# modified although nothing changed (58 of them when this was written - .py,
# .ld, .jdebug, .jflash, .json, the dotfiles). The repository stores LF, and
# this makes every checkout LF too.
#
# text=auto lets git decide what is text (a NUL byte says binary). eol=lf
# checks text out with LF on every platform, so the working tree cannot drift
# from the index and sh/make see the same bytes everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Raw byte blobs: never detect, never convert. The corpus seeds are byte
# patterns and some of them would pass a "is this text" test.
tests/fuzz/corpus/* binary
*.bin binary

# Spelled out on purpose rather than left to the rule above: a CRLF in a shell
# script is not cosmetic. sh reads the \r as part of the command, so `set -e`
# becomes `set -e<CR>` and the script dies with a usage error.
# tests/check_chips.sh had exactly that fate locally, and only escaped notice
# because whichever sh happened to be first on PATH tolerated it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# libFuzzer dictionaries are line-oriented; LF keeps the tokens byte-exact.
*.dict text eol=lf
